'''Ross 128''' is a [[red dwarf]] star in the [[celestial equator|equatorial]] [[zodiac]] [[constellation]] of [[Virgo (constellation)|Virgo]], near [[β Virginis]]. The [[apparent magnitude]] of Ross 128 is 11.13,<ref name="RECONS"/> which is too faint to be seen with the unaided eye. Based upon [[parallax]] measurements, the distance of this star from Earth is {{Convert|11.007|ly|pc|abbr=off|lk=on}}, making it the twelfth [[List of nearest stars and brown dwarfs|closest stellar system]] to the [[Solar System]]. It was first cataloged in 1926 by American astronomer [[Frank Elmore Ross]].<ref name=aj36_856_124/> It is the nearest star in Virgo,<ref name="Kirkpatrick2024"/> and so is occasionally called "Proxima Virginis"<ref name="Bonfils2018"/> by analogy with [[Proxima Centauri]].

==Properties==
[[File:Near-stars-past-future-en.svg|left|thumb|300px|Distances of the [[List of nearest stars|nearest stars]] from 20,000 years ago until 80,000 years in the future]]
This low-mass star has a [[stellar classification]] of M4&nbsp;V,<ref name="baas36"/> which places it among the category of stars known as red dwarfs. It has about 18%<ref name="Pineda2021"/> of the mass of the Sun and 20%<ref name="Pineda2021"/> of the Sun's radius, but generates energy so slowly that it has only 0.033% of the Sun's visible luminosity;<ref name="RECONS"/> however, most of the energy being radiated by the star is in the [[Infrared astronomy|infrared band]], with the [[bolometric luminosity]] being equal to 0.37% of solar.<ref name="Pineda2021"/> This energy is being radiated from the star's [[stellar atmosphere|outer atmosphere]] at an [[effective temperature]] of 3,180&nbsp;K.<ref name="baas36"/> This gives it the cool orange-red glow of an [[M-type star]].

Ross 128 is an [[old disk star]], which means it has a low abundance of elements other than hydrogen and helium, what astronomers term the star's [[metallicity]], and it orbits near the plane of the [[Milky Way]] galaxy.<ref name=sanchez1990/>  The star lacks a strong excess of infrared radiation. An [[infrared excess]] is usually an indicator of a [[debris disk|dust ring]] in orbit around the star.<ref name=apjss154_1_453/><ref name=apj667_1_527/>

[[File:FIVirLightCurve.png|thumb|left|[[Light curve]]s for a flare on FI Virginis, seen in ultraviolet, blue and [[Photometric system#Photometric letters|visual band]] light, adapted from Lee and Hoxie (1972),<ref name=lee/>]]
In 1972, a flare was detected from Ross 128.  It was observed to increase in brightness by about half a magnitude in the [[ultraviolet]] [[UBV photometric system|U]] band, returning to normal brightness in less than an hour.  At optical wavelengths, the brightness changes were almost undetectable.<ref name=lee/>  It was classified as a [[flare star]] and given the [[variable star designation]] FI Virginis.<ref name=ibvs/>  Because of the low rate of flare activity, it is thought to be a magnetically evolved star. That is, there is some evidence that the [[magnetic braking (astronomy)|magnetic braking]] of the star's stellar wind has lowered the frequency of flares, but not the net yield.<ref name=apj1_309_858/>

Brightness variations thought to be due to rotation of the star and magnetic cycles similar to the [[sunspot cycle]] have also been detected.  These cause changes of just a few thousandths of a magnitude.  The rotation period is found to be 165.1 days, and the magnetic cycle length 4.1 years.<ref name=sm/>

Ross 128 is orbiting through the galaxy with an [[orbital eccentricity|eccentricity]] of 0.122, causing its distance from the [[Galactic Center]] to range between {{Convert|26.8|-|34.2|kly|kpc|abbr=on|lk=on}}.<ref name=rmaa34_37/> This orbit will bring the star closer to the [[Solar System]] in the future. The nearest approach will occur in approximately 71,000 years, when it will come within {{Convert|6.233|+/-|0.085|ly|pc|abbr=on|lk=off}}.<ref name=aaa379 />

==Planetary system==
{{main|Ross 128 b}}
[[File:Artist's impression of the planet Ross 128 b.jpg|thumb|left|Artist's impression of the planet [[Ross 128 b]], with the star Ross 128 visible in the background<ref name=eso/>]]
Ross 128 b was discovered in July 2017 by the [[High Accuracy Radial Velocity Planet Searcher|HARPS]] instrument at the [[La Silla Observatory]] in Chile, by measuring changes in [[radial velocity]] of the host star. Its existence was confirmed on 15 November 2017. It is the second-closest known Earth-size exoplanet, after [[Proxima Centauri b|Proxima b]].<ref name="Atlantic">{{cite web|last1=Koren|first1=Marina|title=An Earth-Sized Exoplanet in Our Cosmic Neighborhood|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/science/archive/2017/11/ross-128-planet-discovered/545852/|website=The Atlantic|date=15 November 2017|publisher=The Atlantic Monthly Group.|access-date=15 November 2017}}</ref> Ross 128 b has a [[minimum mass]] 1.4 times that of Earth; a 2019 study predicts a true mass about 1.8 times Earth and a radius about 1.6 times that of the Earth, with large margins of error.<ref name="TaskerLaneuville2020"/> It orbits 20 times closer to its star than Earth orbits the Sun, intercepting only about 1.38 times more solar radiation than Earth,<ref name="Bonfils2018"/><ref>[https://www.bbc.com/news/science-environment-41995572 Nearby planet is 'excellent' target in search for life].  Paul Rincon, ''BBC News''. 15 November 2017.</ref> increasing the chance of retaining an atmosphere over a geological timescale. Ross 128 b is a closely orbiting planet, with a year (orbital period) lasting about 9.9 days.<ref name="Bonfils2018"/><ref name='AstronomyMag'>[http://www2.astronomy.com/news/2017/11/a-potentially-habitable-planet-11-light-years-away A potentially habitable planet has been discovered just 11 light-years away] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180221031045/http://www2.astronomy.com/news/2017/11/a-potentially-habitable-planet-11-light-years-away |date=2018-02-21 }}. John Wenz, ''Astronomy Magazine''. 15 November 2017.</ref> At that close distance from its host star, the planet is most likely [[Tidal locking|tidally locked]], meaning that one side of the planet would have eternal daylight and the other would be in darkness.<ref name=Locked>[https://science.howstuffworks.com/nearby-earth-sized-alien-world-orbits-quiet-star-boosting-habitable-potential.htm Nearby Earth-sized Alien World Orbits 'Quiet' Star, Boosting Habitable Potential]. Ian O'Neill, ''How Stuff Works''. 15 November 2017. Quote: ''"Tidal lock[ing] is expected for Ross 128 b," says Nicola Astudillo-Defru, who works at the Geneva Observatory, University of Geneva in Switzerland, and is co-author of the study.''</ref><ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20000824113823/http://www.solstation.com/stars/ross128.htm Ross 128]. ''Sol Station''. November 2017.</ref> Near-infrared high-resolution spectra from [[Sloan Digital Sky Survey#APO Galactic Evolution Experiment (APOGEE)|APOGEE]] have demonstrated that Ross 128 has near solar metallicity; Ross 128 b therefore most likely contains rock and iron. Furthermore, recent models generated with these data support the conclusion that Ross 128 b is a "temperate exoplanet in the inner edge of the habitable zone."<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Souto|first1=Diogo|last2=Unterborn|first2=Cayman T.|last3=Smith|first3=Verne V.|last4=Cunha|first4=Katia|last5=Teske|first5=Johanna|last6=Covey|first6=Kevin|last7=Bárbara Rojas-Ayala|last8=García-Hernández|first8=D. A.|last9=Stassun|first9=Keivan|date=2018|title=Stellar and Planetary Characterization of the Ross 128 Exoplanetary System from APOGEE Spectra|url=http://stacks.iop.org/2041-8205/860/i=1/a=L15|journal=The Astrophysical Journal Letters|language=en|volume=860|issue=1|pages=L15|doi=10.3847/2041-8213/aac896|issn=2041-8205|arxiv=1805.11633|bibcode=2018ApJ...860L..15S|hdl=10150/628573|s2cid=89612773 |doi-access=free }}</ref>

